      "heading": "Question 4 (a)(i)",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "heading": null,
          "bullets": [
            "Nearly all responses correctly identified the site of crossing over on the figure. Infrequent incorrect responses included labelling the centromere and labelling any point where non-sister chromatids were aligned but not crossing over. Some candidates did not use a labelling line, as required by the question."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "er.q4.a.ii",
      "scope": "part",
      "target": "q4/a.ii",
      "source": {
        "er": {
          "pdfPages": [
            12
          ],
          "printedPage": null
        }
      },
      "heading": "Question 4 (a)(ii)",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "heading": null,
          "bullets": [
            "Responses tended to lack detail in describing how crossing over produces genetic variation in a population. For example, many did not note that crossing over must be between non-sister chromatids to generate variation or referred only vaguely to the exchange of genetic information rather than alleles. References to the exchange of genes in some responses were incorrect. Few candidates linked the formation of genetically variable gametes by meiosis to the production of genetic variation in a population.",
            "A few strong responses correctly described the effect of crossing over on disrupting linkage groups."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "er.q4.b.i",
      "scope": "part",
      "target": "q4/b.i",
      "source": {
        "er": {
          "pdfPages": [
            12
          ],
          "printedPage": null
        }
      },
      "heading": "Question 4 (b)(i)",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "heading": null,
          "bullets": [
            "Explanations of the term F1 generation were mostly limited to referring to the offspring from a parental generation. Fewer candidates noted that the parents of the F1 generation must be homozygous or that the F1 offspring would be heterozygous. Some responses implied that genes were crossed, rather than individuals."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "er.q4.b.ii",
      "scope": "part",
      "target": "q4/b.ii",
      "source": {
        "er": {
          "pdfPages": [
            12
          ],
          "printedPage": null
        }
      },
      "heading": "Question 4 (b)(ii)",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "heading": null,
          "bullets": [
            "The data table for the chi-squared test was completed correctly by most candidates. More frequent incorrect responses included: incorrect rounding applied to the final column, copying across line four of the table into the empty spaces in line three (presumably due the observed numbers being identical), and not summing the four values in the final column."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "er.q4.b.iii",
      "scope": "part",
      "target": "q4/b.iii",
      "source": {
        "er": {
          "pdfPages": [
            12
          ],
          "printedPage": null
        }
      },
      "heading": "Question 4 (b)(iii)",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "heading": null,
          "bullets": [
            "The chi-squared results were generally interpreted correctly, with the key points in the mark scheme observed across many responses. Some responses considered that a calculated value in excess of the critical value meant there was no significant difference between observed and expected results. Other responses included ambiguous comments about the number of degrees of freedom instead of the critical value."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "er.q4.b.iv",
      "scope": "part",
      "target": "q4/b.iv",
      "source": {
